.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH fleetring-db-exec 1  FleetRing "FleetRing manual"
.SH NAME
fleetring\-db\-exec \- Runs one write statement on the database\*(Aqs primary
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBfleetring db exec\fR [\fB\-\-node\fR] [\fB\-\-address\fR] [\fB\-\-control\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-context\fR] [\fB\-\-tls\-dir\fR] [\fB\-\-ca\fR] [\fB\-\-cert\fR] [\fB\-\-key\fR] [\fB\-\-as\fR] [\fB\-\-insecure\-plaintext\fR] [\fB\-\-token\fR] [\fB\-\-token\-file\fR] [\fB\-\-agent\fR] [\fB\-\-log\-format\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-color\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fINAME\fR> <\fISQL\fR> [\fIPARAMS\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Runs one write statement on the database\*(Aqs primary
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-node\fR \fI<NODE>\fR
Sends the request to this data node instead of the primary

The command follows the node\*(Aqs redirect. Its address comes from the control plane\*(Aqs membership.
.TP
\fB\-\-address\fR \fI<ADDRESS>\fR
Sends the request to the data node at `host:port` instead of the primary

The command follows the node\*(Aqs redirect.
.TP
\fB\-\-control\fR \fI<CONTROL>\fR
The control node\*(Aqs address, `host:port`

The node that runs the control role, not any node in the fleet; a data\-only node answers with the control node\*(Aqs address. Defaults to `FLEETRING_CONTROL`, or else the context\*(Aqs `control`, when neither this nor `\-\-config` is given.
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
A daemon\*(Aqs configuration file, to take the control node\*(Aqs address from
.TP
\fB\-\-context\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
Uses this context for every setting it names, instead of the current one

It outranks the environment variables; `FLEETRING_CONTEXT`, and else the current context, rank below them.
.TP
\fB\-\-tls\-dir\fR \fI<DIR>\fR
A directory with `ca.crt` and the client certificates `cert client` writes

`client.crt` and `client.key`, and the `client.<principal>.crt` and `.key` files `\-\-as` picks from. Defaults to `FLEETRING_TLS_DIR`, or else the context\*(Aqs `tls_dir`.
.TP
\fB\-\-ca\fR \fI<CA>\fR
The trusted authority certificates, PEM

Defaults to `FLEETRING_CA`, the context\*(Aqs `ca`, or `ca.crt` in the TLS directory.
.TP
\fB\-\-cert\fR \fI<CERT>\fR
The client certificate, PEM; overrides `\-\-as`

Defaults to `FLEETRING_CERT`, the context\*(Aqs `cert`, or `client.crt` in the TLS directory.
.TP
\fB\-\-key\fR \fI<KEY>\fR
The client certificate\*(Aqs private key, PEM; overrides `\-\-as`

Defaults to `FLEETRING_KEY`, the context\*(Aqs `key`, or `client.key` in the TLS directory.
.TP
\fB\-\-as\fR \fI<PRINCIPAL>\fR
Acts as this principal, with its certificate in the TLS directory

Uses `client.<principal>.crt` and `client.<principal>.key` under the TLS directory, as `cert client <principal>` writes them. An explicit certificate or key at the same step wins over this for that file, and this wins over `client.crt` and `client.key`. Defaults to `FLEETRING_AS`, or else the context\*(Aqs `as`.
.TP
\fB\-\-insecure\-plaintext\fR
Connects in plaintext, to a daemon started with `\-\-insecure\-plaintext`
.TP
\fB\-\-token\fR \fI<TOKEN>\fR
A bearer token from `token issue`, sent with every request

The request then acts with the token\*(Aqs scopes. Defaults to `FLEETRING_TOKEN`, if set.
.TP
\fB\-\-token\-file\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
A file that holds a bearer token, or `\-` for stdin

Defaults to `FLEETRING_TOKEN_FILE`, or else the context\*(Aqs `token_file`.
.TP
\fB\-\-agent\fR \fI<ID>\fR
Acts as the agent with this ID, with the identity minted for it

The identity `fleetring agent add` or `fleetring mcp \-\-agent` minted: `agent\-<id>`, its certificate, and its token, from its directory under the context\*(Aqs agents directory. Defaults to `FLEETRING_AGENT`.
.TP
\fB\-\-log\-format\fR \fI<LOG_FORMAT>\fR
Writes log lines as `text` or `json`

Overrides `FLEETRING_LOG_FORMAT` and the configuration file\*(Aqs `[log] format`.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Prints the result and errors only

No progress, notes, warnings or override warnings. A command with no result then prints nothing.
.TP
\fB\-\-color\fR \fI<COLOR>\fR [default: auto]
When to colour log lines and the picker

`auto` colours on a terminal unless `NO_COLOR` is set or `TERM` is `dumb`; `always` and `never` do as they say.
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
auto: On a terminal, unless `NO_COLOR` is set or `TERM` is `dumb`
.IP \(bu 2
always: Always, even into a pipe or a file
.IP \(bu 2
never: Never
.RE
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fINAME\fR>
The database\*(Aqs name
.TP
<\fISQL\fR>
One SQL statement, with `?` for each parameter
.TP
[\fIPARAMS\fR]
The parameters, in order: `null`, an integer, a decimal number, or text

Prefix one with `text:`, `int:`, `real:`, or `blob:` (hex digits) to choose its type.
